      "writtenOn": "2023-12-07T18:21:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So the `maxLayerDim` should only be updated if there is a filter. The `idealMapping`\u0027s device-to-layer matrix is set to identity as well, so this particular line isn\u0027t doing anything different than just returning `targetOutput` other than handling the cast from DeviceSpace to LayerSpace essentially.\n\nSince `maxLayerDim` is initialized to values larger than `targetOutput`\u0027s dimensions, that should end up not having any affect if there3 was a filter list with just null entries, as we\u0027d want. But we would correctly be getting the aggregate layer bounds needed if one entry accessed a very different part of the coordinate space compared to `targetOutput` (e.g. an offset filter).j\n\nI do think we need to include the `contentBounds` as well. Since `idealMapping` has the same layer matrix as `mapping`, they share the same ParameterSpace-\u003eLayerSpace transform; it\u0027s just the post-device-space mapping that is different. Assuming you go with the plan outlined in the other comment to calculate a `baseLayerBounds`, if you also keep `knownBounds` accessible, you can intersect it with `idealMapping.deviceToLayer(targetOutput)` to get the `idealBaseLayerBounds` to return when there\u0027s no filter.\n\nAnother option that has slightly different semantics around combining the behavior of filters, but is probably not a problem since this is really meant for bounding crazy transform behavior, is to just loop over the `filters` and adjust `maxLayerDim` within the loop. If a particular index is null, just skip it.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 649,
        "startChar": 20,
        "endLine": 650,
        "endChar": 68
      },
      "revId": "60576461d946191c9fc2d6d2a09b292f2bbbde44",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
